Area	Findings	Security Impact
Authentication barriers	Web UI: password ANKODA3F00037785 works for the HTML login form but fails over SSH (password‑auth disabled or key‑only). <br>SSH: password rejected; key‑based login not tested.	Unauthorized remote shell access is mitigated, but the web‑UI credential may be guessable; an attacker who obtains the password (e.g., via phishing) can fully configure the CPE.
Missing standard TR‑181 paths	Device.Users object not present in any response; the CSP has omitted the user‑account data model. <br>Device.Firewall, Device.WLAN objects return 404 or empty payloads.	Simplifies the baseline attack surface (no built‑in user database), but also means the ACS cannot manage local accounts – all provisioning must be done via TR‑069 parameter updates.
Parameter‑set failure codes (observed during CWMP SetParameterValues attempts):<br/>- 7004 – “Parameter not found” (attempted Device.Users.Name).<br/>- 7020 – “Access denied” (attempted Device.X_AIRTEL_LanAccessCfg.SshDisabled set to true without a valid USP session).<br/>- 7021 – “Write‑protect” (tried to modify VLAN IDs or firmware slot).	These codes indicate intentional hardening: unknown parameters are rejected, write‑access is gated behind USP authentication, and critical low‑level settings (VLAN, firmware slot) are locked in read‑only partitions.	 
Hardware intervention requirements	• EDL/test‑point access (see Section 1) enables flashing of any partition, including the active firmware slot. <br>• QDL (Qualcomm Download Tool) + QPST (Qualcomm Product Support Tools) can be used to write a custom modem partition and bypass the Secure‑Boot chain. <br>• Requires physical opening of the case and grounding the EDL GPIO; no remote way to trigger EDL from the existing firmware.	Provides a low‑level bypass for researchers who have physical access. The risk is that a malicious actor with brief physical access could rewrite the bootloader, disable Secure Boot, and install a persistent backdoor.
TR‑069/USP controller exposure	USP TCP 5090 is filtered by the network firewall; however, the CPE responds to Inform messages on the LAN subnet (broadcast). This could be abused for remote code execution if the ACS is reachable from a compromised internal host.	The primary mitigation is network segmentation: place the CPE behind a dedicated VLAN with no route to the internal management network.
